  <h2 id="the-problem">The Problem: Three Fears Every First-Time Course Seller Has (and Pricing Pages Make Worse)</h2>
  <p>
    Spend one evening in course-creator communities and the same three posts repeat:
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>1. "The plan was $29 a month. Why is my first $99 sale worth $88?"</strong> Because the plan price is only the first meter. On Teachable's Starter plan, a $99 course loses $7.43 to Teachable's 7.5% transaction fee and $3.17 to card processing — $88.40 is what reaches you, before the plan's own $29–$39 a month. The "0% fee" platforms run the meter differently: the percentage disappears, but $82–$143 a month of rent is due in months when you sell nothing, and on Thinkific and Kajabi the 0% only holds if you use <em>their</em> checkout — bring your own Stripe account and a 0.5–2% surcharge appears. There is no free option left in this category; there is only the choice of paying per sale, paying per month, or paying both.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>2. "My students are in twelve countries. Who files the VAT?"</strong> This is the question that separates the four more than any feature list. On Teachable Payments, Teachable calculates, collects <strong>and remits</strong> US sales tax and EU/UK VAT for you. Thinkific does the same for the US and Canada — but for UK and EU buyers it only <em>calculates</em> the VAT and hands you a report; remitting it is still your job. On Podia and Kajabi you are the seller throughout: the platforms can calculate and collect tax in places you register, and filing and remitting stay with you. Beginners underestimate this because pricing pages advertise "tax handling" in bold and split that verb in the footnotes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>3. "I made my first sales. When do I actually get the money — and can the deal change under me?"</strong> Every platform here has a gate between "sold" and "paid": Teachable warns new Teachable Payments accounts that initial payouts may take <strong>four to six weeks</strong> while verification completes; Thinkific holds a first payout for 7–14 days; Podia sends money straight to your own Stripe, which is exactly why it has no gate of its own. And layered on top is the loudest complaint pattern of 2025–26: the deal changing after you have built on it. Teachable migrated every school onto new plans in mid-2025 with no option to keep the old ones; Thinkific retired its Basic plan and free plan; Kajabi raised prices in January 2026 without grandfathering; Podia rebuilt itself around a community feed in June 2026 and migrated everyone. The platform you pick is a landlord, and all four landlords just rewrote the lease.
  </p>

