  <h2 id="pricing-tool-by-tool">Pricing, Tool by Tool — With the Real Limits</h2>

  <h3><a href={teachableUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Teachable</a> — the tax department is the product</h3>
  <p>
    Teachable's honest price is not the one in the headline. <strong>Starter is $39/mo ($29 billed yearly) and takes 7.5% of every sale</strong>; the plan most beginners actually need is <strong>Builder at $89/mo ($69 yearly)</strong>, which removes the transaction fee and unlocks running your own affiliate program; <strong>Growth ($189/$139)</strong> adds certificates and removes Teachable branding. There is no free plan — it was retired in the June 2025 restructure — just a 7-day trial. What Teachable sells better than anyone here is the back office: on Teachable Payments it <strong>calculates, collects and remits sales tax and VAT across 45+ markets</strong>, including EU and UK VAT, and the tax is swept out of your account automatically — you never file it. Use your own payment gateway instead and all of that switches off: tax becomes solely your problem.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing: <strong>new Teachable Payments accounts may wait four to six weeks for their initial payouts</strong> while Stripe completes verification, and payouts are withheld once earnings pass $500 if your tax form is not on file. Teachable Payments also layers "bundle" charges and a 0.7% fee on recurring products on top of base processing, so even the 0% plans are not fee-free per sale. Certificates sit on Growth, not Builder. And the trust issue is real: in mid-2025 Teachable retired its old plans and migrated every school onto the new structure with no grandfathering — long-time users report bills jumping from $119 to $309 a month, which is why so many 2024-vintage Teachable reviews describe a product that no longer exists.
  </p>

  <h3><a href={podiaUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Podia</a> — the course business in a box, priced like it</h3>
  <p>
    Podia is the most complete <em>beginner-shaped</em> platform here: courses, digital downloads, coaching, community, a website with a custom domain, and email marketing in every plan. <strong>Mover is $49/mo ($42 billed yearly)</strong> with a <strong>5% transaction fee</strong>; <strong>Shaker is $99/mo ($84 yearly)</strong> with <strong>0% transaction fees</strong>; Earthquaker is $179/$150. Card processing (2.9% + $0.30, via your own Stripe or PayPal) is on top. Because the money flows through <strong>your own</strong> payment accounts, Podia never holds your revenue — there is no Podia payout threshold, hold or review gate. For courses specifically, the basics are not tier bait: drip scheduling, certificates of completion and quizzes (including open-ended questions) are presented as standard course features, students are unlimited on every plan, and we scored Podia higher here than in our <a href="/blog/best-platforms-to-sell-digital-products">digital product platforms comparison</a>, where a one-off file made its subscription harder to justify. The plan caps are the trade: courses count toward Mover's <strong>50-product cap</strong> (500 videos), and only <strong>100 email subscribers</strong> are included before add-on costs.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing: Podia is <strong>not</strong> your merchant of record. It automatically collects sales tax at checkout for jurisdictions <strong>you</strong> register in, across 230 jurisdictions — but registering, filing and remitting stay with you. Podia also rebuilt itself in June 2026 around a built-in community and a unified home feed, migrating all accounts that summer; some long-time creators objected loudly to changes imposed with little warning, and the course builder itself remains deliberately simple (two-level lesson structure, no native mobile app). At beginner volumes Mover is not the cheapest row in the cost table below — it starts winning when the course comes with a community and a list attached.
  </p>

  <h3><a href={thinkificUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Thinkific</a> — the real LMS, at a newly real price</h3>
  <p>
    Thinkific is the most course-serious tool here: <strong>unlimited courses on every plan</strong>, certificates and quizzes included on the entry plan, your own affiliate tools included on every paid plan, and a proper learning-experience toolkit (communities, live sessions, assignments). The catch is what happened to its pricing. The free plan is <strong>retired</strong>, the old Basic plan was withdrawn for new customers in autumn 2026, and the entry plan is now <strong>Start at $109/mo ($82 billed yearly)</strong> — roughly double the old entry price — with Grow at $219/$164 and Expand at $499/$374 above it. The 30-day trial is the longest here. On <strong>Thinkific Payments</strong>, Thinkific charges <strong>0% transaction fees</strong>; process through your own Stripe instead and a <strong>third-party gateway fee applies — 2% on Start</strong> — plus standard processing either way, and a <strong>0.5% tax-solution fee</strong> on sales where tax is assessed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing: Thinkific's tax handling is half a loaf. For sales to US and Canadian students through Thinkific Payments it calculates, collects <strong>and remits</strong> tax automatically; for UK and EU buyers it calculates the VAT and gives you reports, but <strong>you remain responsible for remitting it</strong> — and Thinkific notably never calls itself your merchant of record. Payouts are daily once you are established (US: two business days), but the <strong>first payout is held 7–14 days</strong> for verification. Removing Thinkific's branding waits for Grow, bulk-emailing your learners waits for Grow, and active learners are capped at 10,000 even on Expand. The product is excellent; the beginner maths got much harder in 2026.
  </p>

  <h3><a href={kajabiUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Kajabi</a> — the business platform you grow into, priced like it</h3>
  <p>
    Kajabi is the most complete platform in this comparison and it is not close: courses, coaching, communities, funnels, email marketing, automations, a website and payments under one login. It is also priced for people who already have a business. The public pricing page starts at <strong>Basic, $179/mo ($143 billed yearly)</strong>, with Growth at $249/$199 and Pro at $499/$399; there is no free plan, only a 14-day trial. Kajabi charges <strong>0% revenue share on Kajabi Payments</strong> — use your own Stripe in a region where Kajabi Payments exists and a surcharge applies instead (<strong>2% on Basic</strong>). Basic allows <strong>5 products and 2,500 contacts</strong>; the tools a growing course business actually runs on — <strong>your own affiliate program and advanced automations — are gated to Growth</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Fine print worth knowing: Kajabi says it plainly — <strong>"you remain the merchant of record."</strong> Its Kajabi Sales Tax feature (15¢ per transaction) calculates and collects tax at checkout based on jurisdictions you set up, and its help centre states it "does not file or remit sales taxes on your behalf." Payouts through Kajabi Payments can be daily, weekly or monthly, but update your bank account and <strong>all payouts pause for a mandatory 7-day hold</strong>. Kajabi raised prices in January 2026 — its first rise in nearly a decade, applied to existing customers too — and its partner program, once a famous 30% lifetime deal, now pays new partners a tiered 10–20% (the 30% rate survives only for partners grandfathered before March 2025, whatever the old partner page still advertises). None of this makes Kajabi bad. It makes it a <em>second</em> platform: the one you move to when $143 a month is a rounding error, not a bet.
  </p>

  <h3>The free all-in-one alternative: <a href={systemeAffiliateUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow">Systeme.io</a></h3>
  <p>
    If your plan is "a course <strong>and</strong> an email list <strong>and</strong> a funnel <strong>and</strong> a way to sell the next thing", none of the four above bundles that cheaply — you would be stacking a course platform on top of an email tool on top of a funnel tool. Systeme.io's free plan covers selling with <strong>0% platform transaction fees</strong>, plus funnels, one online course (up to 500 students) and email for up to 2,000 contacts in one dashboard. Its course features are thinner than Thinkific's and its community tools thinner than Podia's — that trade is the whole story. Full breakdown in our <a href="/blog/systeme-io-review">Systeme.io review</a>, and once the sales start, our <a href="/blog/best-email-marketing-for-beginners">beginner email marketing comparison</a> covers the list-building half of the stack.
  </p>

