  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
  <div class="faq-list">
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Is there a free plan anywhere anymore?</h3>
      <p>
        No — and that is the real 2026 story of this category. Teachable retired its free plan in 2025, Thinkific retired its free plan and then its Basic plan, and Podia ended its free plan in October 2024. All four now offer trials (7–30 days) and then charge. The genuinely free way to launch a first course is an all-in-one like Systeme.io (see the note above), accepting thinner course features.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Who handles my sales tax and VAT?</h3>
      <p>
        Teachable, on Teachable Payments, calculates, collects and remits US sales tax and EU/UK VAT across 45+ markets — the most complete answer here. Thinkific collects and remits for US and Canadian sales only; for the UK and EU it calculates VAT but you remit it. On Podia and Kajabi you are the seller: the platforms calculate and collect in jurisdictions you register in, and filing and remitting are your responsibility. For a beginner selling internationally, this difference is bigger than any fee gap in this article.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Which is actually cheapest in year one?</h3>
      <p>
        At roughly $1,000 a month in sales, Teachable Builder ($1,212 all-in) — but only because its fee is 0%; the same company's Starter plan costs $420 <em>more</em> over the year despite a plan price $480 lower. Below roughly $530/mo in sales, Starter's maths flips back; below any sales at all, every subscription here is pure cost, which is why the pre-revenue answer is a free all-in-one or a no-monthly-fee storefront, not a course platform.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>When do I actually get paid?</h3>
      <p>
        Podia: immediately into your own Stripe/PayPal, on the processor's normal timing — no Podia gate at all. Thinkific: daily payouts (US: two business days) after a 7–14 day hold on your first payout. Teachable: daily, weekly or monthly after a two-day delay, but new accounts may wait four to six weeks for initial payouts during verification, and payouts pause past $500 in earnings without a tax form. Kajabi: daily, weekly or monthly via Kajabi Payments, with a mandatory 7-day hold on all payouts if you change your bank account.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can I switch later without losing my students?</h3>
      <p>
        Your student list and course files export from all four. What does not move is the plumbing: drip schedules, quiz settings, payment plans and affiliate setups must be rebuilt, and subscriptions generally have to be recreated. That rebuild cost is exactly what platforms count on when they restructure pricing — all four did so in 2025–26 — so pick the platform whose <em>current</em> deal you would accept again tomorrow.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Why do other articles quote completely different prices for these tools?</h3>
      <p>
        Because they are quoting 2024. Teachable restructured in June 2025, Kajabi raised prices in January 2026, Podia relaunched in June 2026, and Thinkific raised prices in August 2026 and retired its Basic plan that autumn. Any comparison that lists a Thinkific free plan, a $39 Teachable Basic or a $59 Kajabi starter tier is describing platforms that no longer take new customers. Check the date on the review before the prices in it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="bottom-line">Bottom Line</h2>
  <p>
    For a beginner, the decision is not "which platform is best" but "which bill do you want before you know the course sells." Podia charges a moderate rent and a 5% toll, and hands you a whole small business with no feature gates. Teachable charges the least at real volume and quietly removes the tax problem — as long as you skip its cheapest plan entirely. Thinkific charges a newly serious rent for the most serious course toolset, and still leaves EU VAT in your lap. Kajabi charges the most, removes the most other subscriptions, and makes sense only once there is revenue to consolidate.
  </p>
  <p>
    Start where your first job lives, run the fee maths on your real price before you commit, and let outgrowing your first platform be the good problem it is supposed to be.
  </p>
